WEBVTT

00:00:09.327 --> 00:00:10.143
Hi there.

00:00:10.143 --> 00:00:13.791
Welcome to this video where we are going
to learn about different types

00:00:13.791 --> 00:00:16.943
of social engineering attacks
and how to recognize them.

00:00:17.631 --> 00:00:22.783
The goal of this video is to learn
the names of common social engineering attacks.

00:00:22.783 --> 00:00:26.303
When you know the names,
it becomes easier to notice the trick

00:00:26.351 --> 00:00:29.343
and explain what is happening.

00:00:32.863 --> 00:00:35.855
Social engineering
attacks can happen in many ways.

00:00:35.935 --> 00:00:38.815
Some come through email,
some through messages,

00:00:38.815 --> 00:00:41.983
some through phone calls,
and some happen in real life.

00:00:42.543 --> 00:00:46.335
The method changes, but the attacker
is still trying to trick a person.

00:00:47.103 --> 00:00:49.375
Phishing is one of the most common types.

00:00:49.375 --> 00:00:53.023
In phishing, an attacker
sends a fake email that looks real.

00:00:53.663 --> 00:00:57.823
It may pretend to come from a school, 
game shop or online service

00:00:57.935 --> 00:01:00.943
and asks you to click a link 
or share information.

00:01:01.423 --> 00:01:06.815
Smishing is similar to phishing, but it 
happens through text messages or chat apps.

00:01:06.815 --> 00:01:09.615
A smishing message
may say that "a package is waiting",

00:01:09.615 --> 00:01:12.623
"an account has a problem"
or "a prize is ready".

00:01:12.895 --> 00:01:16.143
The goal is usually to make
you tap a dangerous link.

00:01:16.895 --> 00:01:19.103
Vishing means voice phishing.

00:01:19.103 --> 00:01:22.095
This happens through 
a phone call or voice message.

00:01:22.415 --> 00:01:25.263
The attacker may pretend 
to be from a bank, company,

00:01:25.263 --> 00:01:28.543
school, or support team
and ask for private information.

00:01:29.935 --> 00:01:33.103
Impersonation means 
pretending to be someone else.

00:01:33.823 --> 00:01:37.183
An attacker may pretend to be 
a teacher, friend, parent,

00:01:37.215 --> 00:01:40.303
gamer, administrator,
or famous person online.

00:01:41.023 --> 00:01:43.343
The trick works because people often trust

00:01:43.343 --> 00:01:46.335
familiar names or important roles.

00:01:47.295 --> 00:01:49.375
Pretexting is when an attacker

00:01:49.375 --> 00:01:52.847
invents a fake story
to make the request sound believable.

00:01:53.407 --> 00:01:59.695
For example, they may say "I lost access to my account", 
or "I need your code to fix a problem".

00:02:00.079 --> 00:02:03.087
The story is the trap.

00:02:03.695 --> 00:02:05.215
Baiting means offering

00:02:05.215 --> 00:02:08.207
something tempting 
to make people take a risky action.

00:02:08.287 --> 00:02:13.887
The bait could be a free game, skin, 
free coins, a cheat code, a download, or a prize.

00:02:14.527 --> 00:02:17.535
If it sounds too good to be true,
it may be bait.

00:02:19.167 --> 00:02:22.175
Quid pro quo 
means something for something.

00:02:22.527 --> 00:02:25.615
In this attack,
someone offers help or a reward

00:02:25.695 --> 00:02:28.575
in exchange for information or access.

00:02:28.575 --> 00:02:32.815
For example, I will give you free coins
if you tell me your login code.

00:02:34.543 --> 00:02:36.863
Scareware tries to frighten people.

00:02:36.863 --> 00:02:41.614
A pop up or message may say your device 
has a virus or your account will be deleted.

00:02:42.142 --> 00:02:45.902
It then tells you to click quickly,
install something, or pay money.

00:02:47.278 --> 00:02:51.438
Fake support scams happen when attackers
pretend to be technical support.

00:02:51.550 --> 00:02:55.390
They may say they can fix your account,
computer game or phone.

00:02:56.158 --> 00:03:00.270
Real support teams should not ask
for your password or private codes.

00:03:01.966 --> 00:03:04.974
Some social engineering happens offline.

00:03:05.086 --> 00:03:07.614
Shoulder surfing
is when someone watches your screen

00:03:07.614 --> 00:03:10.606
or keyboard to see your password or pin.

00:03:10.974 --> 00:03:18.334
Tailgating is when someone follows another 
person into a protected area without permission.

00:03:20.478 --> 00:03:23.150
Let's compare the types:

00:03:23.150 --> 00:03:24.798
Phishing uses e-mail.

00:03:24.798 --> 00:03:27.182
Smishing uses messages.

00:03:27.182 --> 00:03:29.422
Vishing uses calls.

00:03:29.422 --> 00:03:32.414
Impersonation uses fake identity.

00:03:32.910 --> 00:03:35.150
Pretexting uses a fake story.

00:03:36.590 --> 00:03:39.582
Baiting uses tempting offers.

00:03:40.318 --> 00:03:43.310
Scareware uses fear.

00:03:43.582 --> 00:03:44.590
Great work!

00:03:44.590 --> 00:03:47.902
You now know several types
of social engineering attacks.

00:03:49.038 --> 00:03:53.054
Remember, the type may change,
but the safe response is similar.

00:03:53.646 --> 00:03:55.614
Slow down. Check carefully.

00:03:55.614 --> 00:04:01.646
Do not share private information
and ask a trusted adult when unsure.

00:04:01.694 --> 00:04:03.262
Thank you for watching.

00:04:03.262 --> 00:04:06.206
Do not forget to watch
other videos in our series.